{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And HaShem plagued Pharaoh.</b> The view of most commentators is that He brought actual plagues upon him. And similarly with Avimelekh there occurred a closing of the bodily orifices, and this is the sense of the verse (I Chronicles 16:21): ״לא הניח לאיש לעשקם ויוכח עליהם מלכים״ — He allowed no man to oppress them, and rebuked kings on their account — and these are Pharaoh and Avimelekh, whom He rebuked with plagues. But the view of the Gaon Rabbeinu Saadya, of blessed memory, is that the word וינגע means 'threatened to bring,' not that He actually brought; for were it so, Pharaoh would be found wronged in his judgment, since the princes brought her to his palace and he did not touch her and sinned with her not at all, and since he did not sin — why punishment? And likewise is his view regarding Avimelekh (Genesis 20:18): ״כי עצור עצר ה׳״ — that He threatened them with closing of the orifices, not that He did so to them. And what it says, ״וירפא״ — 'and He healed' — does not mean that illness had preceded, but rather the guarding so that illness not come at all is called healing, in the manner of (Exodus 15:26): ״כי אני ה׳ רופאך״ — for I am HaShem your healer — and the verse ״ויוכח״ is to be understood according to his view as threat and speech, not as deed. But not so is the view of our Rabbis, of blessed memory; rather, both kings intended evil and were stricken with plagues and closing of the orifices, according to the plain sense of the verses."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:14.906775370Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}